[38;2;124;124;124m  s⁺ = min{ t ∈ S : t > s }[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhich exists because {t ∈ S : t > s} is a nonempty subset of the well-ordered [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mset S.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey observation: For any s₁ < s₂ in S (both non-maximal), the open intervals [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(s₁, s₁⁺) and (s₂, s₂⁺) are disjoint. Indeed, since s₂ ∈ S and s₂ > s₁, we have [0m[38;2;124;124;124ms₂ ≥ s₁⁺ (as s₁⁺ is the least element of S greater than s₁). Hence s₁⁺ ≤ s₂ < [0m
[38;2;124;124;124ms₂⁺, so (s₁, s₁⁺) ∩ (s₂, s₂⁺) = ∅.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, each interval (s, s⁺) is a nonempty open interval in ℝ (nonempty because s [0m[38;2;124;124;124m< s⁺), so it contains a rational number. By the density of ℚ in ℝ, for each non-[0m[38;2;124;124;124mmaximal s ∈ S, choose q_s ∈ ℚ ∩ (s, s⁺).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince the intervals (s, s⁺) are pairwise disjoint, the chosen rationals q_s are [0m[38;2;124;124;124mall distinct. Thus the map s ↦ q_s is an injection from S \ {max S} (if max [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexists) into ℚ.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince ℚ is countable, S \ {max S} is countable, and therefore S is countable.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis shows every well-ordered subset of ℝ is countable, so no well-ordered [0m
[38;2;124;124;124muncountable set of real numbers exists. ∎[0m

